WEBVTT

1
. --> .
Before we touch the worked example, notice what students often forget about a measurement.

2
. --> .
A number is not automatically trustworthy just because it looks neat.

3
. --> .
The reading only deserves trust when the tool, the scale divisions, and the method can actually support it.

4
. --> .
A rough ruler, a fine caliper, and a micrometer screw gauge can all look at the same object, but they do not justify the same amount of detail.

5
. --> .
This lesson is about that hidden question behind every reading.

6
. --> .
How much precision does the instrument really support, and what uncertainty should an honest report include?

7
. --> .
Start with the key word resolution.

8
. --> .
Resolution means the smallest change an instrument can show clearly.

9
. --> .
On a simple ruler, that might be one millimetre.

10
. --> .
On a caliper, the scale can show a much finer change.

11
. --> .
On a micrometer screw gauge, the detail can become finer again.

12
. --> .
That matters because the instrument sets the smallest trustworthy difference you can really claim to have seen.

13
. --> .
So when the scale is coarse, your reading must stay modest.

14
. --> .
When the scale is fine, the reading can carry more justified detail.

15
. --> .
One of the best ways to feel this lesson is to keep one object fixed and swap the tool.

16
. --> .
Imagine measuring a small wire or a marble diameter.

17
. --> .
A metre rule can give you a rough classroom reading.

18
. --> .
A vernier caliper can usually do better because its scale is finer.

19
. --> .
A micrometer screw gauge is built for very small thicknesses and can usually support the finest resolution of the three.

20
. --> .
So the object stays the same, but the trustworthy level of detail changes with the tool.

21
. --> .
This leads to a second habit.

22
. --> .
Read to the smallest clear division, estimate carefully between marks when appropriate, and stop there.

23
. --> .
Never decorate the answer with extra digits that the scale could not truly support.

24
. --> .
If the ruler gives you one-millimetre spacing, the final report should reflect that limitation.

25
. --> .
A calculator or a tidy-looking decimal does not give you extra measurement truth.

26
. --> .
The instrument decides what level of detail is justified.

27
. --> .
Now add the partner idea: uncertainty.

28
. --> .
Uncertainty tells the reader how closely the reported value can reasonably be trusted.

29
. --> .
For a simple scale reading, a common estimate is about half the smallest division.

30
. --> .
So if the smallest ruler division is one millimetre, a reasonable uncertainty is often plus or minus zero point five millimetres.

31
. --> .
That does not make the measurement weak.

32
. --> .
It makes the report honest.

33
. --> .
The goal of good physics is not to pretend a measurement is exact.

34
. --> .
The goal is to state clearly what the tool genuinely supports.

35
. --> .
The lesson also separates different types of error.

36
. --> .
Random error is the one that makes repeated readings wobble above and below the best estimate.

37
. --> .
It can come from tiny reading differences, small timing delays, or natural variation in the method.

38
. --> .
Because the scatter changes from one reading to the next, taking several readings and averaging them can reduce the effect.

39
. --> .
So random error is about spread, not a fixed one-way shift.

40
. --> .
Systematic error behaves differently.

41
. --> .
Instead of scattering around the best value, it pushes every reading the same way.

42
. --> .
A balance that always reads zero point two grams too high shows this kind of bias.

43
. --> .
That is often called zero error.

44
. --> .
If you simply average those readings, the bias stays there.

45
. --> .
So systematic error needs checking, zero correction, or calibration.

46
. --> .
That is why the lesson teaches different responses for scatter and for fixed offsets.

47
. --> .
Pause and check your own thinking.

48
. --> .
Which tool is usually more trustworthy for a tiny thickness: a rough ruler or a caliper?

49
. --> .
The caliper, because its finer divisions support smaller uncertainty.

50
. --> .
If a ruler has one-millimetre divisions, what uncertainty is often reasonable?

51
. --> .
About plus or minus zero point five millimetres.

52
. --> .
And if a balance always reads too high by the same amount, is that random error or systematic error?

53
. --> .
It is systematic error.

54
. --> .
You are now ready for the worked example in this lesson.

55
. --> .
The guided example starts with a ruler whose smallest division is one millimetre and a line that reads six point four centimetres.

56
. --> .
Read it in a clean sequence.

57
. --> .
First, identify the smallest division on the instrument.

58
. --> .
Second, take about half of that as a reasonable simple-scale uncertainty.

59
. --> .
Third, express that uncertainty in the same unit as the main reading.

60
. --> .
So one millimetre becomes plus or minus zero point five millimetres, which is the same as plus or minus zero point zero five centimetres.

61
. --> .
If that chain already feels clear, the worked example will feel like confirmation rather than a surprise.
